nt of the drug  left on the skin  unabsorbed at the end so why do some  things absorb through the skin into the  bloodstream  well it mostly depends on what the  substance is which is why that blanket  60  number looks so dodgy to people who  study pharmacology  firstly there's the size of the molecule  the smaller it is  the more easily it squeezes between all  the stuff that your skin is made of  sort of like how you try to streamline  yourself when you get through a crowd  your skin is also made up of watery and  oily parts  things that dissolve in water tend not  to dissolve in oil  and so a substance has to be the right  balance of water and oil soluble  to get into the skin and then also leave  the skin and get into the blood  so as you expect the drugs that are in  transdermal medications tend to tick off  all these boxes  and things that aren't don't and it's  the same for other things that touch  your skin  your skin also doesn't have the same  permeability all over  and so the amount that gets absorbed  also depends on what part you're talking  about  this table over here shows how well  three different substances absorb  in different parts of your body if you  look across the table  then you can see there's a massive  variation in how well different  substances absorb  if you look down the table you can see  the massive range depending on what part  you're putting on some parts of your  body are better at absorbing things so  your armpits your ear canal your jaw  your scrotum other parts of the body  like the foot  your palms your forearms are less  permeable  one thing to note about this table is  this is percutaneous absorption so that  just means absorbing through the skin  not necessarily getting to your blood  there is a lot of stuff between the  surface of your skin  and your blood you can see this on my  handy model the surface of your skin is  up here you apply the stuff here and  then it has to diffuse through all these  layers before it hits the blood  so only a fraction of the stuff you put  on the surface of the skin will actually  make it down to the blood  and that fraction depends on a lot of  things like the blood flow in that area  how thick these layers are you might  also remember the fda studies that got  reported as  sunscreen soak all the way into your  bloodstream the abstract of the first  study had a bunch of percentages and  some people thought that those  percentages were the amounts absorbed  they weren't they were actually the  coefficients of variation which is  a measure of how repeatable the  measurements were but you can actually  roughly calculate  what percentage does get absorbed into  the blood from the data in the paper a  redditor did these calculations  and it turns out that for avobenzone  0.011  gets absorbed into the blood plasma then  doesn't that mean skincare doesn't work  science woman  skincare doesn't have to get that deep  and not at that higher concentration  most skincare ingredients actually work  in the epidermis which is in these top  few layers so when you put the skincare  on your skin  it doesn't have to absorb all the way  into the bloodstream a lot of skincare  ingredients actually work really  effectively in the stratum corneum which  is this very top layer  if you want something to get into your  blood and work somewhere else then it  has to get through all of these layers  and then once it hits the blood it gets  diluted because we have about five  liters of blood  that's about 13 of these i mentioned  this before but there are formulating  tricks that can get things to get into  the skin they're used in transdermal  patches and creams  they're also used in some skin care  products i mentioned covering stuff up  with a patch  that stops the ingredients from getting  rubbed away plus it also keeps moisture  in and moisture helps disturb the skin a  bit  and help things absorb better there's  also penetration enhancers which are  ingredients that help skin